<article class="env-remark" id="0H1F">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H1F">Remark <span data-tag="0H1F">33.43.9</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be a regular curve over $k$. By Lemmas <a href="/tag/0B8Y" data-tag="0B8Y">33.43.8</a> and <a href="/tag/0BXW" data-tag="0BXW">33.43.6</a> there exists a nonsingular projective curve $\overline{X}$ which is a compactification of $X$, i.e., there exists an open immersion $j : X \to \overline{X}$ such that the complement consists of a finite number of closed points. If $k$ is perfect, then $X$ and $\overline{X}$ are smooth over $k$ and $\overline{X}$ is a smooth projective compactification of $X$. </p>
</article>